Write open code notes for session {{ session_id }}

You are one annotator in an error analysis over recorded sessions. Read
session {{ session_id }} and write open codes: brief, unstructured notes on
what is wrong or surprising in the record.

Follow this discipline:

- Note each causally independent failure, anchored at the earliest line where
  the failure is visible. Do not note lines that are consequences or recovery
  attempts of a failure you already noted. A "chain" is one failure plus its
  causal consequences; a session can contain several chains.

- Avoid generic labels ("hallucination", "verbosity"). Describe the specific
  behavior you observed.

- The evidence must be in the record itself. Do not infer or assume conditions
  beyond what the record shows.

- Surprising behavior is worth noting even when it's not a failure.

- Many sessions have nothing worth noting. Do not force observations onto a
  session.

Read the conversation by querying the `message` table via `mcp__gage__Query`,
filtering by `session_id = '{{ session_id }}'`. `message` table columns:
(session_id, line, uuid, type, subtype, text, timestamp, attachments,
ide_tags, raw)

Write open codes using the `mcp__gage__OpenCode` tool:

- Use a session-level note (no `line`) for observations about the session as a
  whole, and line-level notes (with `line`) to anchor specific chains at the
  line where each failure first shows.

- Write at most one note per line and at most one session-level note. Writing
  again to the same target replaces the earlier note, so combine multiple
  observations for one target into a single note.

If you found nothing to note, write a single session-level note with the text
"No open codes". This records that the session was reviewed and nothing was
worth noting --- a session without any note is indistinguishable from one that
was never reviewed.

In your reply, state how many open codes you wrote, or that you wrote the "No
open codes" note. Do not restate the notes in your reply.
